  "text": "Priscilla corrected a famous preacher in private. Acts eighteen. A man named Apollos comes to Ephesus. Born at Alexandria, eloquent, mighty in the scriptures, fervent in spirit. He teaches diligently the things of the Lord. And then the text adds one qualifier that changes everything. Knowing only the baptism of John. He is brilliant and he is behind. So he starts speaking boldly in the synagogue. And Aquila and Priscilla hear him. And they do not stand up and correct him in front of the room. Whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly. They took him aside. They filled in what was missing. That is the whole move. Afterwards Apollos goes on to Achaia with a letter of recommendation from the believers, and he helps them much, and mightily convinces the Jews publicly. Small note that readers have made much of. In several places her name is listed first, including here. Luke never tells us why, so treat the theories as theories. What we can see is that she taught the teacher.",
